As applicable, create a structured and dynamic song using Suno AI, use meta tags enclosed in square brackets [ ] to define specific sections, styles, and musical elements. Begin by organizing your song with structural tags, such as [Intro] to introduce the track, followed by [Verse] to establish the lyrical narrative. Use [Pre-Chorus] to build momentum leading into the [Chorus], which serves as the main, memorable refrain. If needed, follow the chorus with a [Post-Chorus] to reinforce its impact. Incorporate a [Bridge] to contrast the main sections and add variety. Use [Hook] to highlight catchy phrases or instrumental motifs, and include [Break] or [Interlude] for instrumental transitions. Conclude the song with an [Outro], a [Refrain] for repeated lyrical lines, a [Big Finish] for a grand ending, [End] to mark the final note, or [Fade Out] for a gradual decrease in volume. To shape the song's musical identity, use instrumental and style tags that specify the genre or mood. For example, choose [Acoustic] for a raw, unplugged feel, [Ambient] for atmospheric soundscapes, or [EDM] for electronic dance music. Genres like [Hip Hop], [R&B], [Rock], [Reggae], [Jazz], or [Blues] add distinct musical flavors. Opt for regional styles such as [Atlanta Rap] or international influences like [J-Pop] and [K-Pop]. Use specific instrument tags such as [Piano], [Drums], [Cello], or [Synth] to highlight individual instruments. To set the song's emotional tone, apply tags like [Romantic] or [Party]. Combine multiple tags to craft unique and cohesive songs. For instance, an acoustic ballad could be structured as [Intro][Acoustic][Verse][Chorus][Bridge][Outro], while a high-energy party track might include [Intro][EDM][Verse][Chorus][Break][Outro]. Experiment with various combinations to achieve the desired mood, genre, and thematic content. 

